  emit-process-spawn-on-core-helper : CodegenState -> CodegenState
  emit-process-spawn-on-core-helper (st) =
   let fn-off = st.code-len
   in let n = list-push (st.fo-names) "process-spawn-on-core"
   in let o = list-push (st.fo-offsets) fn-off
   in let st0 = __record-set (__record-set st "fo-names" n) "fo-offsets" o
   in let st1 = st-append-code st0 (li reg-rax spawn-affinity-addr)
   in let st2 = st-append-code st1 (mov-store reg-rax reg-rsi 0)
   in emit-jmp-to st2 "process-spawn"

 Both wakes are a CMPXCHG off BLOCKED, not a plain store, because both can
 fire for the same waiter: the child's exit runs the wake loop at the same
 moment the waiter's own recheck sees the child already dead. If each side
 plain-stored, the self-rescue could set RUNNING and return -- running the
 waiter live on its core -- while the wake loop then stamped READY over it,
 and a third core would claim that READY and resume the waiter's saved
 context: one process on two cores. So the wake loop claims BLOCKED -> READY
 and the self-rescue claims BLOCKED -> RUNNING, each conditional on the slot
 still being BLOCKED; exactly one transition wins. If the self-rescue loses,
 the waiter is already READY and must NOT run on -- it relinquishes the core
 to __idle_dispatch and is resumed normally, returning through its saved
 context exactly as a lost wake would have.

